20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] ## General Rules
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] ## Subagents
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - Avoid subagents. They are "too clever by half".
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] ---
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] ## Communication
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] ## Definition: Claims vs Hypotheses
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - Label uncertain reasoning as Hypothesis; only use Claim with explicit evidence.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - Claims must cite evidence (code refs, logs, metrics). Otherwise, downgrade to Hypothesis.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - For each Hypothesis, include:
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........]   - Hypothesis: concise, falsifiable statement.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........]   - Predictions: measurable outcomes if true.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........]   - Test: minimal experiment (setup, variables, metrics, pass/fail).
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........]   - Confounders: likely alternatives and controls.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - Language:
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........]   - Hypothesis: “suggests”, “may”, “indicates”.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........]   - Claim (with evidence): “shows”, “demonstrates”, “causes”.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - Label any statement which is a hypothesis as such.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - The use plan,`./.malvin/logs/20260529_151312_pkoxvuii/request.md`, when present, overrides ADVICE. ADVICE is not binding.
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] ## Shorthand
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - DCC: Don't Change Code
20260529.111315.421 [>kpop..........] - RL: Be sure to look at recent logs.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] ## Style
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] When communicating to the user:
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - No corporate-speak (e.g., "learnings", "close the loop")
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - No cheezy dev-speak (e.g., "bolt that on", "fire-and-forget", "duct tape")
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - No colloquialisms
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - Write in clear, plain language.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - Use complete sentences.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] # Definition: KPop
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] [
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] KPop is short for "Karl Popper".
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] You may or may not be solving a repo coding problem, so the Repo Coding Rules may or may not apply.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] KPop may be referenced later on like a command, "KPop: <problem statement or question>"
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] ]
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] Apply this method to the user's problem.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] Restate the problem clearly.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] Repeat until you think you've solved the problem:
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] LOOP_START
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - **Brainstorm**: To get fresh, creative ideas, run `malvin invent IDEAS_PROMPT`, where you specify the `IDEAS_PROMPT`. Call this on at least one iteration, but don't wait until the end when it's too late to make use of the ideas.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........]   		  It should take no more than 30s to return, so don't worry about the time. It'll be a worthwhile investment. Use the ideas to help generate new hypotheses.
20260529.111315.422 [>kpop..........] - **Hypothesize**: Hypothesize one falsifiable explanation of the cause of the problem.
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] - **Predict**: Define a falsifying test. If the hypothesis were true, what outcome would the test produce?
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] - **Falsify**: Run the test. If falsified, reject the hypothesis.
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] LOOP_END
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] Log your hypotheses and test results -- as they become available -- to `./.malvin/logs/20260529_151312_pkoxvuii/_kpop/exp_log_20260529_151312_pkoxvuii.md`. Be sure to log hypotheses and results
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] as generate them. They are valuable. The user and other agents will want to read them.
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] When you are all done, append a brief executive summary and a super-brief tl;dr to the log, and echo both to the user (the chat/context) directly.
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] # This KPOP turn
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] Complete up to `10` KPOP iterations in this turn. Before beginning each iteration, write a section header `## Step K — KPOP …` to `./.malvin/logs/20260529_151312_pkoxvuii/_kpop/exp_log_20260529_151312_pkoxvuii.md`; use the next integer K that is not already used in that file.
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] 
20260529.111315.423 [>kpop..........] If you solve the problem before using the iterations budget, append a section `## KPOP_SOLVED` with one short paragraph explaining why, and stop adding new steps.
